The main question was whether HDAC7 actually contributes to cancer aggressiveness or is simply associated with it. We first found that HDAC7 was highly expressed in aggressive cancer models. We then inhibited HDAC7 and looked at the effects on cancer cells, gene expression, and chromatin. We found changes in cancer stemness, cell behavior, and repressive histone marks. This supported a functional role for HDAC7 rather than just a correlation.
